  "response": "Read all four sites plus `handleError`, the callers, and the worker wiring. Here's what's actually true.\n\n## The four `swallowed_error` findings\n\n### 1. `crime_00019` — `src/tsc.ts:215` (`runTypeScriptCompiler`)\n\nEvidence, quoted:\n> \"protected operation: `const start = Date.now()` (+6 more statement(s))\"\n> \"handler kind: try/catch (line 215)\"\n> \"what happens to the failure: the handler logs a message but never passes the error, so the stack, cause chain, and error code are lost\"\n> \"missing signal: no rethrow or rejected promise; a log call is present but the error value is not passed to it; no typed error result the caller could branch on; the error is never inspected\"\n\n**Not recoverable. This is the one that hides the failure.** The error being caught here is the one `emitDtsFiles` raises deliberately after printing every TS diagnostic (`src/tsc.ts:155-160`): `throw new Error('TypeScript compilation failed')`. The catch discards it and the function — declared to return `getExports(...)`, i.e. `ExportDeclaration[]` — falls off the end returning `undefined`. Then `src/index.ts:199-200`:\n\n```ts\nconst exports = runTypeScriptCompiler(options)\nawait runDtsRollup(options, exports)\n```\n\n`runDtsRollup` hits `if (!exports) throw new Error('Unexpected internal error: dts exports is not define')` — so a user's real type error is relabelled as an internal tsup bug, and that relabelled error is then swallowed by finding #2. `experimentalDtsTask` resolves, `Promise.all([dtsTask(), mainTasks()])` resolves, `build()` resolves. Anything calling `build()` programmatically (4 direct importers) is told the build succeeded.\n\nOne correction to the detector's evidence: the stack is *not* lost. `handleError(error)` prints `error.stack` to stderr and sets `process.exitCode = 1` (`src/errors.ts:31-34`). What's lost is **control flow** — the caller cannot branch, the promise resolves, and the only surviving failure signal is a mutable global that no caller reads.\n\n### 2. `crime_00018` — `src/api-extractor.ts:197` (`runDtsRollup`)\n\n> \"calls inside the protected region: Date.now, Math.floor, logger.info, cleanDtsFiles, rollupDtsFiles, logger.success\"\n> \"what happens to the failure: the handler logs a message but never passes the error, so the stack, cause chain, and error code are lost\"\n\n**Not recoverable — same shape, one step downstream.** Identical `handleError(error); logger.error('dts', 'Build error')` with no rethrow. It swallows both the bogus internal error from #1 and any genuine `rollupDtsFiles` failure. Also main-thread, so no worker channel either. Second priority.\n\n### 3. `crime_00033` — `src/rollup.ts:187` (`runRollup`)\n\n> \"calls inside the protected region: Date.now, Math.floor, logger.info, rollup, Promise.all, options.outputConfig.map\"\n> \"missing signal: no rethrow or rejected promise; a log call is present but the error value is not passed to it\"\n\n**Not recoverable, but it currently fails loudly by accident.** `runRollup` never throws, so its caller's own handler is dead code:\n\n```ts\ntry {\n  await runRollup(config)\n  parentPort?.postMessage('success')\n} catch {\n  parentPort?.postMessage('error')          // unreachable\n}\n```\n\nThe main thread still learns about the failure only because `handleError` has a side channel — `if (!isMainThread && parentPort) parentPort.postMessage('error')` — which fires before the `'success'` message, so `src/index.ts:241-243` rejects with `new Error('error occurred in dts build')` first. That correctness rests entirely on message ordering inside an unrelated helper. Anyone refactoring `handleError` to drop the `parentPort` branch silently turns dts failures green. Fix third: the fix is to make the real `catch` reachable, not to add anything new.\n\n### 4. `crime_00049` — `src/utils.ts:135` (`jsoncParse`)\n\n> \"protected operation: `return new Function(\\`return ${strip(data).trim()}\\`)()`\"\n> \"what happens to the failure: the handler returns `{}` without inspecting the error\"\n> \"the catch declares no binding, so the error object is not even available to the handler\"\n\n**Genuinely a deliberate fallback, but the wrong one — and it is silent in a way the others aren't.** The in-code justification is `// Silently ignore any error / That's what tsc/jsonc-parser did after all`. That's a real precedent for *not throwing*, and `{}` is a usable empty-config value, so this is the only one of the four where a fallback is defensible. But unlike the other three there is no `handleError`, no log line, and no binding — a malformed `tsconfig.json` becomes an empty config, `baseUrl`/`paths`/`target` silently revert to defaults, and the build emits wrong-but-successful output. It has the widest blast radius in the scan (`\"blast_radius_direct_importers\": 18`). Fix by keeping the fallback and adding a warning with the error and the file path — don't make it throw; `loadTsConfig` callers depend on the tolerant behaviour.\n\n## Fix first: `src/tsc.ts:215`\n\nIt's the head of the chain. It swallows the *only* precise diagnostic in the experimentalDts path, and because it returns `undefined` from a function typed to return `ExportDeclaration[]`, it actively manufactures a misleading second error downstream. Fixing it alone stops tsc failures from reporting success, and removes the false \"Unexpected internal error\" that sends anyone debugging this in the wrong direction.\n\nReplacement — note `start`/`getDuration` move above the `try` so the duration is still available in the handler, and the return type becomes explicit so TS enforces that every path returns:\n\n```ts\nexport function runTypeScriptCompiler(\n  options: NormalizedOptions,\n): ExportDeclaration[] {\n  const start = Date.now()\n  const getDuration = () => {\n    return `${Math.floor(Date.now() - start)}ms`\n  }\n  try {\n    logger.info('tsc', 'Build start')\n    const dtsOptions = options.experimentalDts!\n    const exports = emit(dtsOptions.compilerOptions, options.tsconfig)\n    logger.success('tsc', `⚡️ Build success in ${getDuration()}`)\n    return exports\n  } catch (error) {\n    // Report where it failed, then let the caller decide — `build()` must not\n    // resolve when declaration emit failed.\n    logger.error('tsc', `Build error in ${getDuration()}`)\n    throw error\n  }\n}\n```\n\n`handleError` is intentionally dropped from this catch: it is already installed at the process boundary (`main().catch(handleError)` in `src/cli-default.ts:5` and `src/cli-node.ts:7`), so the stack and `process.exitCode = 1` still happen exactly once, and `build()` now rejects instead of resolving. `ExportDeclaration` is already imported as a type at `src/tsc.ts:7`, so no import change is needed.\n\nOne follow-on this exposes rather than fixes: with `runTypeScriptCompiler` throwing, `runDtsRollup`'s `if (!exports)` guard becomes unreachable dead code, and `runDtsRollup`'s own catch (#2) still swallows `rollupDtsFiles` failures — so #2 needs the same treatment before the experimentalDts path is fully honest.",
